{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":220641,"duration_api_ms":221787,"ttft_ms":188904,"ttft_stream_ms":1285,"time_to_request_ms":12,"num_turns":2,"result":"{\"basis\":\"Source-only adversarial read of the supplied diff plus the quoted current specref_findings; tools disabled, nothing executed, no test result relied upon. Scope limited to the coordinator startup refusal and the canonical-needs SpecRef repair. Both prior blocking findings are closed by the code as shown: names_carrier (spine_carrier.py:475-486) no longer folds backslashes, so plan_briefs._canonical_need_ref and check_trajectory.specref_findings give the same answer for a backslash spelling on POSIX (composer: no carrier match; R-E: non-existent path), and the single './' regression is asserted alongside '././' rather than replaced. The coordinator guard (agent_loop.py:4502) is mode-independent and sits ahead of resolve_coordinator_dials, with a regression that fails if setup runs first, so the reported claim that drive/worker/interactive all refuse holds for every path that reaches main. APPROVE rather than CHANGES_REQUESTED because every residual divergence I can construct has the composer as the strict side (it refuses) and R-E as the silent side — no input makes both readers pass a wrong row — and because the reachable half of finding 1 (Windows backslashes) is caught by the POSIX leg of the existing CI matrix. The findings below are each a one-line-to-small fix and are follow-up work, not a reason to hold this diff.\",\"findings\":[\"MINOR — The composer does not strip the path half while R-E does, so a leading-space SpecRef cell still splits the two readers. check_trajectory does `pathpart, frag = pathpart.strip(), frag.strip()` before calling names_carrier; plan_briefs._canonical_need_ref (plan_briefs.py:279) passes the raw partition result straight in, and PurePosixPath(' docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml') keeps the leading space, so it matches no carrier and the plan runner refuses the scope while R-E judges the row normally. A space in a hand-edited CSV cell is far more plausible than any of the exotic spellings the repair already covers. Fix is one line — strip pathpart in _canonical_need_ref — and it makes the shared helper's normalization contract actually shared.\",\"MINOR — Aliased spellings of the canonical needs carrier now fall through to a clean result instead of a finding. names_carrier is deliberately lexical: it collapses './' but does not resolve '..', and a backslash stays a filename byte. So `docs/requirements/../requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml#SN-999` (all platforms) and `docs\\\\requirements\\\\stakeholder-needs.toml#SN-999` (Windows, where root / pathpart does resolve) reach an existing file, miss names_carrier, and land in the anchor arm — which the diff's own test_specref_does_not_treat_nested_config_tables_as_registry_rows proves returns clean for a TOML target. Before the narrowing, registry_ids flagged both as 'names no such row'. The composer refuses both, so nothing passes on both readers, and the POSIX CI leg rejects the backslash form; but this is the canonical needs carrier under an alias, not one of the other registries the narrowing deliberately released. Either record the acceptance in the resync record alongside the non-need-TOML scope, or close it in R-E by comparing resolved target identity against the resolved carrier paths before falling through to the anchor arm.\",\"MINOR — The canonical needs path still has two operands even though the comparison is now shared. check_trajectory passes SN_CSV, plan_briefs passes NEEDS_REL; they agree only because carriers()/stem() discards the suffix. The repair unified how a carrier reference is compared but left what it is compared against restated in two modules — the same single-home smell that produced the original H1/R-E disagreement. Export the canonical needs registry path from spine_carrier next to NEED_CARRIERS and have both call sites read it, or pin it with a test asserting the two constants share a stem. Related nit: SN_CSV is a stale name for a carrier set that is .toml/.md.\",\"MINOR — R-E has no test for the single './' spelling, which is the documented failure mode. tests/test_hats.py now parametrizes './' and '././' for the composer, but on the R-E side the only normalized-path coverage is the '././' prefix embedded in test_empty_need_registry_still_exposes_an_empty_row_id_set — which also conflates two behaviors under a name that mentions only the empty registry, so a normalization regression would fail a test whose name points at the wrong cause. Mirror the composer parametrization with a dedicated R-E case on './docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml#SN-9'.\",\"MINOR — The startup regression covers drive and worker but not interactive, which is the mode the review note asserts rather than tests. The guard is mode-independent and sits after _resolve_root, so I believe the claim; but the test's whole shape is a parametrization over coordinator modes, and the third tuple costs one line. Note also that because _resolve_root is monkeypatched to succeed, the test does not establish the ordering between root-resolution errors and the identity refusal — that precedence (bad --root wins) is fine, just unpinned.\",\"MINOR / unverifiable from the diff — the comment at agent_common.py:~138 that the prior review quoted as 'before either coordinator loop starts' is not in this diff. If that wording survives it now undercounts (three executable modes) and names no enforcing site; point it at the agent_loop.main guard so the next auditor of this contract greps one name instead of re-deriving the entry-point census.\"],\"verdict\":\"APPROVE\"}","stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"ef985678-0931-4924-9156-6fbbd4d9d33c","total_cost_usd":0.5857885,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17097,"cache_read_input_tokens":3733,"output_tokens":16197,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17097,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":16197,"cache_read_input_tokens":3733,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17097,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17097},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":7922,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.008017,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":16197,"cacheReadInputTokens":3733,"cacheCreationInputTokens":17097,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.5777715,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"basis":"Source-only adversarial read of the supplied diff plus the quoted current specref_findings; tools disabled, nothing executed, no test result relied upon. Scope limited to the coordinator startup refusal and the canonical-needs SpecRef repair. Both prior blocking findings are closed by the code as shown: names_carrier (spine_carrier.py:475-486) no longer folds backslashes, so plan_briefs._canonical_need_ref and check_trajectory.specref_findings give the same answer for a backslash spelling on POSIX (composer: no carrier match; R-E: non-existent path), and the single './' regression is asserted alongside '././' rather than replaced. The coordinator guard (agent_loop.py:4502) is mode-independent and sits ahead of resolve_coordinator_dials, with a regression that fails if setup runs first, so the reported claim that drive/worker/interactive all refuse holds for every path that reaches main. APPROVE rather than CHANGES_REQUESTED because every residual divergence I can construct has the composer as the strict side (it refuses) and R-E as the silent side — no input mak